        "comments": {
          "__diagram__": [
            {
              "id": "a1",
              "text": "This runs on every OnBattery change, once at daemon startup, and whenever the tray toggle fires. Both guards short-circuit to the tray update, so the common case writes nothing at all.",
              "ts": 1786953600000
            }
          ],
          "event": [
            {
              "id": "e1",
              "text": "Lid open/close also lands here: LidIsClosed sits on the same UPower interface, so the handler fires more often than plug events alone. That is why the two guards matter.",
              "ts": 1786953600000
            }
          ],
          "held": [
            {
              "id": "h1",
              "text": "Reads ActiveProfileHolds. Writing ActiveProfile releases every hold, which would cancel GNOME's low-battery power-saver protection \u2014 so a held profile is left untouched. The cost: after the hold releases, the profile is not corrected until the next plug event.",
              "ts": 1786953600000
            }
          ],
          "toggle": [
            {
              "id": "g1",
              "text": "GNOME's appindicator extension only runs its double-click path when the tray item exports an Activate method (indicatorStatusIcon.js: _maybeHandleDoubleClick checks supportsActivation). libayatana-appindicator exports Scroll, SecondaryActivate and XAyatanaSecondaryActivate but no Activate, so powerswitch publishes its own StatusNotifierItem at /StatusNotifierItem and leaves the AppIndicator one PASSIVE \u2014 hidden by the shell, but still exporting the dbusmenu our item points at. Activate (double click) and SecondaryActivate (middle click) both land in powerswitch:131.",
              "ts": 1786996800000
            }
          ],
          "flipcfg": [
            {
              "id": "g2",
              "text": "toggle() in powerswitch:201 rewrites the entry for whichever state is live \u2014 ac when plugged in, battery when not \u2014 flipping the profile actually in force between performance and balanced, saving it, then calling apply(). The change persists, so it survives the next unplug and shows up in the settings window.",
              "ts": 1786996800000
            }
          ],
          "readcfg": [
            {
              "id": "c1",
              "text": "Re-read from ~/.config/powerswitch.json on every event, so a change in the settings window takes effect without restarting the daemon. Anything unparseable or out of range falls back to the defaults.",
              "ts": 1786953600000
            }
          ],
          "tray": [
            {
              "id": "t1",
              "text": "Always reached, on every path. The icon shows the profile actually in force, not the configured one, so a held profile displays honestly.",
              "ts": 1786953600000
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    }
